# transientCoupledMultiTurbulentProfileInertia: the two brackets of 5.40 on a
# shaped inlet -- which knob moves which one.
#
#   Src -> A -> { X1 (3D RANS duct) || By (1D pipe) } -> X2 (3D RANS duct) -> B -> Snk
#
# The topology, the two meshes, the probes, the wall functions, the operating
# point, the mis-stated proxy bores, the dt and the eight levels are 5.40's. What
# changes is the shape the interface writes on the inlet faces: 5.40 ran eight
# arms with flat plugs and its own boundary 2 recorded that the shape axis was
# not crossed with attribution at all (and 5.39's boundary recorded the same for
# `parab_x2pin`). Here every arm is parabolic except one flat control, so the
# crossing is the whole point of the case.
#
# 5.40 settled the structure of the row on a stepping edge:
#       rowError * dpBlock = (R*q - dpFriction) + (M*dq/dt - dpInertia)
#   \_______ chord _______/   \____ attribution bill ____/
# and found the two brackets sit three orders of magnitude apart (X1 +0.0472 Pa
# against +718.87 Pa at level 1) and are owned by different things: the interface
# re-linearises R every pass, so the chord bracket is tolerance-sized whatever the
# source, while `slab` cancels the bill because its number IS the one the block
# measured its drop over. The question this case asks is which bracket the INLET
# SHAPE acts on -- because if shape moves only the chord, the attribution ladder
# is a property of the declared M and of nothing else, and if it moves the bill
# too then "whose inertance" and "what the block did" are not separable.
#
# Seven arms. The first three are 5.40's flat attribution ladder, kept here so the
# ladder and the shaped rungs live in one log:
#   f_net     -- both edges stepping, drawing bore, flat       (= 5.40's nn_both)
#   f_region  -- ... the region's own number, flat             (= 5.40's pp_both)
#   f_slab    -- ... the slab the drop was measured over, flat (= 5.40's ss_both)
# The next two are the shaped rungs of the same ladder, so the source moves with
# the shape held and the shape moves with the source held:
#   p_net     -- shaped, both edges stepping, drawing bore
#   p_region  -- shaped, both edges stepping, region's number
# The last two put both per-edge tables on the SAME edge under a shaped inlet,
# which is 5.40's es_mix/se_mix crossed with shape; p_pin is the `parab_x2pin` cell
# 5.39 recorded as missing, now also carrying a per-edge source:
#   p_mix     -- X1 steps on the drawing, X2 stays quasi on its slab
#   p_pin     -- the mirror: X1 stays quasi on the drawing, X2 steps on its slab
# X2's quasi edge in p_mix is 5.40's boundary 1 -- a quasi-steady edge charged with
# the slab's inertance, which the tables allow (attribution is installed before the
# time loop, so it reaches a quasi edge exactly as far as a stepping one) and which
# no case has ever run: its row pays M*dq/dt and its grade is 0 by definition.
# There is no shaped arm with `slab` on the stepping FREE edge, and that is a
# measurement, not an omission: the same case written with p_slab (slab on both
# stepping edges, shaped) stops inside level 1 with
#   "the detailed region reports a non-positive drop (-89.475517 Pa) at 0.000022
#    m3/s"
# -- a source three times too small is survivable on a flat plug (f_slab runs to
# its 30 passes) and is not survivable when the same rate arrives as a Poiseuille
# profile. So slab is placed where its number does not drive the split.
# `ddtFrom` and `inertiaFrom` are per-edge dicts keyed by starting-set name and the
# shape is a per-edge list in `starts.profile`; every arm's effective scheme,
# source AND shape are read back from the log rather than assumed from the case.
fluid { rho 1000; mu 1.0e-3; }

# Src, J1, J2 and J3 are incompressible ports (no bulkModulus), so the branch
# split is a result of the momentum balance alone rather than of node compliance.
nodes {
    Src  { volume 1.0; liquidFraction 0.5; pressure 1.0e6; }
    J1   { volume 1.0; liquidFraction 0.5; pressure 1.0e6; }
    J2   { volume 1.0; liquidFraction 0.5; pressure 1.0e6; }
    J3   { volume 1.0; liquidFraction 0.5; pressure 1.0e6; }
    Snk  { fixedPressure 1.0e6; }
}

sources {
    # 6.0e-5 m3/s, as in 5.36..5.40: the operating point at which both
    # wall-function ducts keep their first cell centre above the y+ = 11 their log
    # law is written for at every level and every shape.
    feed { node Src; rate 6.0e-5; }
}

pipes {
    A  { from Src; to J1; length 0.3;  diameter 0.02; }

    # X1 sits in the parallel position (its rate is what the split relaxes), X2 in
    # the series position (its rate is pinned by topology to the injected flow).
    # Both proxy bores stay deliberately short -- X1 0.005 against the exact
    # 0.0056418958354775631, X2 0.006 against 0.00638307648642292245 -- so the
    # drawing overstates the region by 1.273x and 1.132x and the three sources are
    # three different numbers, which is what makes attribution visible at all.
    X1 { from J1; to J2; length 0.2;  diameter 0.005;
         coupledResistance 1.4e7; }
    By { from J1; to J2; length 0.3;  diameter 0.008; }

    X2 { from J2; to J3; length 0.24; diameter 0.006;
         coupledResistance 1.36e7; }

    B  { from J3; to Snk; length 0.3; diameter 0.02; }
}

# dt 0.2 s over eight levels -- 5.38/5.39/5.40's level, kept so that the only
# variables are the two per-edge tables and the inlet shape.
time { dt 0.2; endTime 1.6; reportEvery 1; }

couple {
    edges ( X1 X2 );
    relax 1.0;
    # 1e-3 as in 5.38/5.39/5.40: the measured floor for a shaped inlet on this
    # partner. The tolerance reads |R*q - chord| / chord, and chord on a stepping
    # edge is the friction half, so it is blind to the installed inertance; what
    # shape does to the tolerance is the separate record 5.39 left.
    tolerance 1e-3;
    maxSubIterations 12;
    # the interface operator is not a variable here -- 5.33 crossed du/dt with
    # quasi-Newton and 5.37 crossed shape with it. Every arm walks block GS.
    accelerates ( 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 );
    # per-ARM ddt word: every arm says Euler, which is also what both blocks' own
    # schemes{ ddt } say, and the ddtFrom table below takes one edge of p_mix and
    # one edge of p_pin back to steady. So every `steady` in this case is a table
    # beating a block that asked for du/dt -- the direction 5.39 established.
    ddts        ( Euler Euler Euler Euler Euler Euler Euler );
    ddtFrom {
        p_mix { X1 Euler;  X2 steady; }
        p_pin { X1 steady; X2 Euler;  }
    }
    # per-ARM inertia source: the three flat rungs of the ladder, the two shaped
    # rungs, then the drawing on both mixed arms -- and both mixed arms override
    # their PINNED edge to that edge's slab below, which beats this word edge by
    # edge. The free edge keeps the drawing in both, because it is the edge whose
    # rate the split relaxes and a three-times-too-small M on it will not survive a
    # shaped inlet (see the header).
    inertias    ( network partner slab network partner network network );
    inertiaFrom {
        p_mix { X1 network; X2 slab; }
        p_pin { X1 network; X2 slab; }
    }
    starts {
        # Every set names a shape per edge next to its starting coefficients, and
        # the seeds are the control arm's own secants at this rate -- the same
        # 1.4e7 / 1.36e7 in all seven arms, so neither table nor shape can come in
        # through the starting coefficients.
        f_net     { X1 1.4e7; X2 1.36e7; profile ( flat flat ); }
        f_region  { X1 1.4e7; X2 1.36e7; profile ( flat flat ); }
        f_slab    { X1 1.4e7; X2 1.36e7; profile ( flat flat ); }
        p_net     { X1 1.4e7; X2 1.36e7; profile parabolic; }
        p_region  { X1 1.4e7; X2 1.36e7; profile parabolic; }
        p_mix     { X1 1.4e7; X2 1.36e7; profile parabolic; }
        p_pin     { X1 1.4e7; X2 1.36e7; profile parabolic; }
    }
}

details {
    # X1: 0.2 x 0.005 x 0.005 m, 8 cells across the gap so the first cell centre
    # sits inside the log-law band. Probe = the last 0.07 m of a 0.2 m region, so
    # its slab inertance is 0.35 of the region's.
    X1 {
        block {
            min   (0 0 0);
            max   (0.2 0.005 0.005);
            cells (20 8 2);

            xmin  { name inlet;   type inlet;    }
            xmax  { name outlet;  type outlet;   }
            ymin  { name bottom;  type wall;     }
            ymax  { name top;     type wall;     }
            zmin  { name front;   type symmetry; }
            zmax  { name back;    type symmetry; }
        }
        probe { min 0.12; max 0.19; }
        turbulence { model kOmegaSST; intensity 0.05; lengthScale 3.0e-4;
                     relaxation 0.7; }
        schemes { ddt Euler; div upwind; laplacian corrected; grad corrected; }
        simple { maxIterations 300; continuityTol 3e-3; pcorrTol 1e-4;
                 momentumTol 1e-4; relaxU 0.9; relaxP 0.3; pRelaxStart 0.02; }
        vtk out_transient_turbulent_profile_inertia_X1.vtu;
    }

    # X2: a different aspect ratio -- 4 mm gap, 8 mm span -- so its resistance is
    # not X1's scaled. Probe = the last 0.07 m of a 0.24 m region, so its slab is
    # 0.291667 of the region's inertance.
    X2 {
        block {
            min   (0 0 0);
            max   (0.24 0.004 0.008);
            cells (24 8 2);

            xmin  { name inlet;   type inlet;    }
            xmax  { name outlet;  type outlet;   }
            ymin  { name bottom;  type wall;     }
            ymax  { name top;     type wall;     }
            zmin  { name front;   type symmetry; }
            zmax  { name back;    type symmetry; }
        }
        probe { min 0.16; max 0.23; }
        turbulence { model kOmegaSST; intensity 0.05; lengthScale 2.4e-4;
                     relaxation 0.7; }
        schemes { ddt Euler; div upwind; laplacian corrected; grad corrected; }
        simple { maxIterations 300; continuityTol 3e-3; pcorrTol 1e-4;
                 momentumTol 1e-4; relaxU 0.9; relaxP 0.3; pRelaxStart 0.02; }
        vtk out_transient_turbulent_profile_inertia_X2.vtu;
    }
}
